  "hints": [
    "this is a small variation on 3Sum - what changes when you're chasing 'closest' instead of 'exactly zero'?",
    "once sorted, for a fixed first element, moving the two inner pointers toward each other still makes sense - the sum increases when you move left forward, decreases when you move right backward",
    "at every step, compare the current triplet's sum to the target and keep the closest one seen so far",
    "if the current sum equals the target exactly, you can stop early - it can't get any closer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "returning the triplet instead of the sum",
    "not sorting first, which breaks the two-pointer movement logic",
    "comparing absolute difference incorrectly, or forgetting to update the best answer even when the current sum is worse in one direction but closer overall"
  ],
